{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LANDED — Gerrit **1724** MERGED (`a147945aae`), `LLM-Review +1` + `Verified +1` first time.\n\n## A follow-up change (1729) because ONE AC named a test I had replaced with a CI gate\nAuditing the ACs against merged main rather than against my own memory of the work: the last AC names `tests/unit/test_notice_records_pandoc_gpl.py` explicitly, and I had implemented that check as a CI workflow step instead. The CI gate is genuinely useful — it fails on a loosened pin, a multi-version pin, a missing NOTICE, or a NOTICE that no longer names the pinned wheel or its licence — but it is CI-ONLY, so the first signal of drift is a `Verified -1` after a push, and the reasoning lives in shell rather than beside the assertions.\n\n1729 adds the named test. It deliberately does NOT check the version twice independently: `pyproject.toml` and `NOTICE` are asserted to AGREE, because the interesting failure here is not a missing NOTICE (loud, obvious) but a pin bump that quietly leaves the record describing a binary nobody distributes any more. Two independent version checks would both pass in exactly that case. It also asserts the pin is a single `==` rather than a range (an unknowable version cannot be recorded at all) and that NOTICE says pandoc arrives only via the optional `wiki` extra (without which the record would overstate the obligation for installs shipping no pandoc).\n\n## AC test-name drift, recorded so the ticket matches reality\nThe other six ACs are all satisfied, but four named tests that do not exist under those names — the plan was written before the tests were. The description has been updated to the real names rather than left citing fictions:\n- `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` → the timeout half is `test_the_timeout_default_matches_the_config_default` / `test_the_timeout_is_read_from_config` / `test_a_non_positive_timeout_falls_back_rather_than_disabling_rendering`; the REAP half is `test_a_timeout_reaps_the_whole_process_group_not_just_the_child` + `test_the_child_leads_its_own_process_group`.\n- `test_dc_pandoc_error_degrades` → `test_a_timed_out_unit_degrades_to_its_original_markdown`, `test_a_nonzero_exit_degrades_without_raising`, `test_an_unspawnable_pandoc_degrades_without_raising`.\n- `test_dc_hardening_does_not_change_output` → `test_rendered_output_is_byte_identical_to_the_landed_renderer`.\n\nSplitting each into its separate property was the right call — `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` as written would have had to assert the timeout, the group reap AND the fallback in one body, and the group-reap assertion is the one that must not be diluted.\n\nAll 7 ACs are now ticked. Remaining before close: 1729 green and submitted."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786620953684992001,"uuid":"18251ac5-012b-4df9-9a0b-902cc69e4f8c"}